= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $x>0$, use <integration by parts> with $u=\ln x$ and $dv=dx$. Then
$$
\boxed{\int\ln x\,dx=x\ln x-\int1\,dx=x\ln x-x+C.}
$$
Differentiating the answer checks cancellation of the extra constant term from $(x\ln x)'$.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Gaussian function> $e^{-x^2}$ is even and $\sin x$ is odd, so their product is an <odd function>. Moreover $|e^{-x^2}\sin x|\leq e^{-x^2}$, whose integral on the real <line> is finite. Thus the <improper integral> is absolutely convergent, not merely a symmetric principal value, and the contributions of its two half-lines cancel:
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-x^2}\sin x\,dx=0.}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

For the <parametric curve>, $dx/dt=e^t\ne0$ and $dy/dt=\cos t$. Therefore $dy/dx=e^{-t}\cos t$. The condition $x=e^t=1$ gives $t=0$, so
$$
\boxed{\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{x=1}=1.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Use <completing the square> in each coordinate:
$$
(x-3)^2-9+(y-1)^2-1+1=0,
\qquad (x-3)^2+(y-1)^2=9.
$$
Comparing with the standard <circle> equation gives
$$
\boxed{\text{centre }(3,1),\qquad\text{radius }3.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Let the contact point have positive horizontal coordinate $x_0$. Equality of slopes between the <tangent line> and the logarithmic <curve> requires $k=1/x_0$. The point must also satisfy both equations, giving $\ln x_0=kx_0+1=2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{x_0=e^2,\qquad k=e^{-2}.}
$$
The contact point is $(e^2,2)$, and the resulting <line> $y=e^{-2}x+1$ has exactly the required <derivative> there.

