= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <domain of a function> excludes $x=3$. Both branches are positive, with a vertical <asymptote> at $x=3$ approached from both sides with value $+\infty$, and horizontal <asymptote> $y=0$ at either infinity. The graph is symmetric about $x=3$ because it depends only on $|x-3|$.

For $x<3$, the formula is $2/(3-x)$ and the <derivative> is positive; for $x>3$, it is $2/(x-3)$ and the <derivative> is negative. It passes through $(0,2/3)$ and has no zeros.

\Image[paper-1-reciprocal-distance.png]
{title=The two positive reciprocal-distance branches and their vertical and horizontal asymptotes}

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <sine addition formula> gives $(\sin x+\cos x)/\sqrt2=\sin(x+\pi/4)$. On the requested <interval> the shifted angle runs from $0$ to $\pi/2$, so the graph rises from $(-\pi/4,0)$ through $(0,1/\sqrt2)$ to $(\pi/4,1)$.

Its <derivative> $\cos(x+\pi/4)$ is nonnegative and reaches zero only at the right endpoint. Its second <derivative> is $-\sin(x+\pi/4)\leq0$, so the rising arc is concave downward and ends with a horizontal tangent.

\Image[paper-1-shifted-sine.png]
{title=A rising concave-down quarter sine wave on the specified interval}

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

Isolate the <square root>: $\sqrt{2x^2+17}=3-x$. Its right side must be nonnegative, so retain the condition $x\leq3$ before squaring. Squaring then gives
$$
2x^2+17=(3-x)^2,\qquad x^2+6x+8=(x+2)(x+4)=0.
$$
Both candidates pass the original equation: $\sqrt{25}-2=3$ and $\sqrt{49}-4=3$. Thus
$$
\boxed{x=-2,\quad x=-4.}
$$
The substitution check excludes the possibility of extraneous roots introduced by squaring.

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

Use $2\cos^2x=1+\cos2x$ to reduce the equation to $\sin2x=\cos2x$. Equivalently, $\sqrt2\sin(2x-\pi/4)=0$, so $x=\pi/8+k\pi/2$ for an integer $k$. Restricting to $[0,\pi]$ gives
$$
\boxed{x=\frac\pi8,\qquad x=\frac{5\pi}{8}.}
$$

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=11}

The first four terms form the cubic <Taylor polynomial> about $x_0$:
$$
\boxed{f(x_0)+f'(x_0)(x-x_0)+\frac{f''(x_0)}{2!}(x-x_0)^2
+\frac{f^{(3)}(x_0)}{3!}(x-x_0)^3.}
$$
With a continuous fourth <derivative> nearby, <Taylor's theorem> gives a remainder $O((x-x_0)^4)$. Continuous <derivatives> through third order suffice for the weaker remainder $o((x-x_0)^3)$. These are local approximations; equality with an infinite <Taylor series> requires additional convergence information.

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $f(x)=x^{1/4}$, the first two <derivatives> are $f'(x)=\tfrac14x^{-3/4}$ and $f''(x)=-\tfrac3{16}x^{-7/4}$. At $16$ they give
$$
f(16)=2,\qquad f'(16)=\frac1{32},\qquad f''(16)=-\frac3{2048}.
$$
Use the first three <Taylor expansion> terms with increment $17-16=1$:
$$
\boxed{17^{1/4}\approx2+\frac1{32}-\frac3{4096}=2.030517578125.}
$$
The positive third <derivative> shows that this quadratic approximation underestimates the true value. The <Taylor remainder> is at most $7/262144\approx2.67\times10^{-5}$, since $f^{(3)}(x)=(21/64)x^{-11/4}$ decreases on $[16,17]$ and the cubic remainder is $f^{(3)}(\xi)/6$.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply the <Maclaurin series> for the exponential and the <sine>, keeping terms through degree three:
$$
e^x\sin x=\left(1+x+\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^3}{6}+O(x^4)\right)
\left(x-\frac{x^3}{6}+O(x^5)\right).
$$
Only $x$, $x^2$, and $(1/2-1/6)x^3$ contribute at these orders, so
$$
\boxed{e^x\sin x=x+x^2+\frac{x^3}{3}+O(x^4).}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $v=x+x^2$ in the <logarithmic series> $\ln(1+v)=v-v^2/2+v^3/3+O(v^4)$. Since $v^2=x^2+2x^3+O(x^4)$ and $v^3=x^3+O(x^4)$,
$$
\ln(1+x+x^2)=x+x^2-\frac12(x^2+2x^3)+\frac13x^3+O(x^4).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\ln(1+x+x^2)=x+\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{2x^3}{3}+O(x^4).}
$$

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use independent choices with constant <probabilities> $p$ and $q=1-p$. Completing the entire reverse circuit while the forward-moving object never moves requires the first $F$ choices all to be reverse moves. Thus
$$
\boxed{P=q^F=(1-p)^F.}
$$
Here and below, a zero-length product is interpreted as one, including at $p=0$ or $p=1$.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

After $j$ choices, let $C_j$ be the number of forward moves. The two unwrapped positions are $C_j$ and $F-(j-C_j)$, whose gap is $F-j$. It follows that the first subsequent meeting occurs exactly at step $F$, regardless of the order of the choices. Its <position> is the number of forward moves in that block.

That count has a <binomial distribution> with parameters $F,p$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_n^{\rm meet}=\binom Fn p^nq^{F-n},\qquad 0\leq n\leq F.}
$$
The two endpoint labels $0$ and $F$ represent the same physical seat reached by different complete circuits; the unwrapped convention distinguishes their <probabilities>.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The meeting <position> $N$ is the sum of $F$ independent <Bernoulli random variables>, each of <mean> $p$ and <variance> $pq$. <Linearity of expectation> and <variance additivity for independent random variables> give
$$
\boxed{\mu=Fp,\qquad\sigma^2=Fpq.}
$$
These are the <mean> and <variance> of the unwrapped integer label specified in the question, rather than circular angular statistics.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the forward-moving object to have been waiting at the meeting <position>, the last move must be a reverse move. The preceding $F-1$ choices must then contain exactly $n$ forward moves and $F-n-1$ reverse moves. Choosing their order gives
$$
\boxed{p_n^{\rm specialMeet}=\binom{F-1}{n}p^nq^{F-n},\qquad 0\leq n\leq F-1.}
$$
At $n=F$ the <probability> is **zero**, since the reverse-moving object was already at that unwrapped <position> initially. At $n=0$ the displayed formula is $q^F$, consistent with the forward object waiting at its initial <position> throughout. Equivalently, use the binomial convention $\binom{F-1}{F}=0$ to include this endpoint in the same formula without a negative power of $q$.

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $N$ be the meeting label. If $N>n$, the forward-moving object has already crossed $n$ while the reverse-moving object has not yet reached it, so the forward object wins the race to $n$. If $N<n$, the reverse object passed $n$ first. If $N=n$, the required event is exactly the special meeting from part (d).

These disjoint possibilities give
$$
\boxed{P(\text{forward object reaches }n\text{ first})
=p_n^{\rm specialMeet}+\sum_{k=n+1}^{F}p_k^{\rm meet}.}
$$
This includes the endpoints: for $n=0$ the <probability> is $1$, and for $n=F$ it is $0$, with the initial positions understood in the unwrapped convention.

\Image[paper-1-stopping-race.png]
{title=Example paths in a race between fixed success and failure targets}

= f
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

First take $1\leq n\leq F-1$. The forward object reaches $n$ at its $n$th move, while the reverse object reaches that label at its $(F-n)$th move. If the forward object wins after $k$ reverse moves, then $0\leq k\leq F-n-1$. The final choice is forward; among the preceding $n+k-1$ choices, exactly $k$ are reverse. By the <negative binomial stopping argument>, this case has <probability> $\binom{n-1+k}{k}p^nq^k$.

Sum these mutually exclusive cases and equate the result to part (e), using parts (b) and (d). This proves the <success-failure stopping identity> here:
$$
\sum_{k=0}^{F-n-1}\binom{n-1+k}{k}p^nq^k
=\binom{F-1}{n}p^nq^{F-n}
+\sum_{k=n+1}^{F}\binom Fk p^kq^{F-k}.
$$
The right side splits the same race into a special meeting at $n$ and all meetings beyond $n$; the left side splits it according to the number of reverse moves at the winning time.

At $n=F$, both sides are zero with an <empty sum> and $\binom{F-1}{F}=0$. At $n=0$, the event has <probability> one; the right side equals $\sum_{k=0}^{F}\binom Fk p^kq^{F-k}=1$. The printed left side includes $\binom{-1}{0}$ and is outside the usual nonnegative-upper-index combinatorial definition. With the explicit generalized convention **$\binom{-1}{0}=1$**, its $k=0$ term is one and all $k\geq1$ terms vanish, so the identity also holds at that endpoint. Without this convention the displayed sum is undefined there; the zero-target race must instead be stated separately. All identities extend to $p=0,1$ by their <polynomial> forms.

= 13
{parent=Paper 1}
