= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Compute the <characteristic polynomial> using $\det(\lambda I-M)$:
$$
\det(\lambda I-M)=\lambda^3-7\lambda^2+6
=(\lambda-1)(\lambda^2-6\lambda-6).
$$
Its three distinct real roots are $1$ and $3\pm\sqrt{15}$. For $\lambda=1$, the first row of $(M-I)v=0$ gives $z=3y$ and the second then gives $x=y$, so take $v_1=(1,1,3)^T$.

For either root of $\lambda^2-6\lambda-6=0$, take $z=1$ and solve the row equations to get $v=(\lambda-8,5-\lambda,1)^T$. Substitution makes the first two row residuals opposite multiples of the quadratic equation and the third zero. Thus a complete choice is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\lambda_1&=1,&v_1&=(1,1,3)^T,\\
\lambda_-&=3-\sqrt{15},&v_-&=(-5-\sqrt{15},\ 2+\sqrt{15},\ 1)^T,\\
\lambda_+&=3+\sqrt{15},&v_+&=(-5+\sqrt{15},\ 2-\sqrt{15},\ 1)^T.
\end{aligned}}
$$
Every nonzero <scalar> multiple is another valid <eigenvector> in the corresponding one-dimensional <eigenspace>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For either sign, the <dot product> with $v_1$ is
$$
v_1\cdot v_\pm=(-5\pm\sqrt{15})+(2\mp\sqrt{15})+3=0.
$$
The remaining pair also satisfies
$$
v_+\cdot v_-=(25-15)+(4-15)+1=\boxed{0}.
$$
Hence the three <eigenvectors> are mutually <orthogonal>. This also follows from the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices>, since $M=M^T$ and its <eigenvalues> are distinct, but the displayed products verify the actual chosen <vectors> directly.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the <linear system> as $Ar=d$ with
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\3&4&5\\1&3&4\end{pmatrix},\qquad
r=\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix},\qquad
d=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\\3\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Expansion along the first row gives $\det A=(16-15)-2(12-5)+3(9-4)=2$. Since **$\det A=2\ne0$**, $A$ is an <invertible matrix>, and $r=A^{-1}d$ is the unique solution for this right side.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <adjugate matrix> formula gives
$$
A^{-1}=\frac12\begin{pmatrix}1&1&-2\\-7&1&4\\5&-1&-2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Multiplication by the right-side column yields
$$
r=A^{-1}d=\frac12\begin{pmatrix}1+2-6\\-7+2+12\\5-2-6\end{pmatrix},
\qquad\boxed{(x,y,z)=\left(-\frac32,\frac72,-\frac32\right).}
$$
Multiplying the inverse by $A$ checks the <identity matrix>, and substitution of this solution into the three original rows gives $1,2,3$ respectively.

