Multiply by the <complex conjugate> of the denominator:
$$
\frac{2+3i}{3+2i}=\frac{(2+3i)(3-2i)}{13}=\frac{12+5i}{13}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\Re z=\frac{12}{13},\qquad\Im z=\frac5{13}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The logarithm's argument is $\sqrt3/2+i/2=e^{i\pi/6}$, with <complex modulus> one. The principal <complex logarithm> is therefore $\ln1+i\pi/6=i\pi/6$, giving
$$
\boxed{\Re\operatorname{Log}z=0,\qquad\Im\operatorname{Log}z=\frac\pi6.}
$$
If $\ln z$ denotes all logarithm values rather than the principal branch, they are $i(\pi/6+2\pi k)$ for $k\in\mathbb Z$: the <real part> remains zero and the <imaginary part> changes by integer multiples of $2\pi$. The principal value is the conventional single-valued answer.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Integer powers of $i$ repeat with <period> four. Thus $i^{-5}=i^{-4}i^{-1}=1/i=-i$, and
$$
\boxed{\Re z=0,\qquad\Im z=-1.}
$$
This negative integer power is single-valued and does not require a logarithm branch.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The number being squared has <polar form of a complex number> $e^{2\pi i/3}$. Squaring doubles its argument, giving $e^{4\pi i/3}=-1/2-i\sqrt3/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\Re z=-\frac12,\qquad\Im z=-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.}
$$

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Rearrange to $z^4=1+i\sqrt3=2e^{i\pi/3}$. A fourth root has modulus $2^{1/4}$ and an argument one quarter of $\pi/3+2\pi k$. Therefore all four solutions are
$$
\boxed{z_k=2^{1/4}e^{i(\pi/12+k\pi/2)},\qquad k=0,1,2,3.}
$$
Their arguments differ by $\pi/2$, so they are distinct. Raising any of them to the fourth power returns $1+i\sqrt3$.

= 15
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $d=b\cdot x$ and $B=|b|^2$. Taking the <dot product> of the original equation with $b$ eliminates $b\times x$ and gives $d=a\cdot b-Bd$. Thus
$$
\boxed{b\cdot x=\frac{a\cdot b}{1+|b|^2}.}
$$
Next take its <cross product> with $b$. The term $d b$ disappears, and the <vector triple product identity> gives
$$
b\times x=b\times a-b\times(b\times x)
=b\times a-bd+Bx.
$$
Insert this expression back into $x=a-db-b\times x$. The two longitudinal terms cancel, leaving $(1+B)x=a-b\times a$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{x=\frac{a-b\times a}{1+|b|^2}=\frac{a+a\times b}{1+|b|^2}.}
$$
The denominator is strictly positive for every real $b$, including $b=0$. Substitution using the same triple-product identity verifies the solution and establishes uniqueness. This is the <cross-product operator with a longitudinal rank-one term>.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $d=b\cdot x$. Taking a <dot product> with $b$ reduces the <vector> equation to $(1-b\cdot c)d=b\cdot a$. If $b\cdot c\ne1$, this determines a unique <scalar> and hence a unique <vector>:
$$
\boxed{x=a+\frac{b\cdot a}{1-b\cdot c}\,c.}
$$
Direct substitution verifies it, so necessity has also produced sufficiency. The formula includes $c=0$, when $x=a$.

If $b\cdot c=1$, the reduced <scalar> equation becomes $0=b\cdot a$. When $b\cdot a\ne0$, **there is no solution**. When $b\cdot a=0$, **all solutions are $x=a+dc$ with arbitrary $d\in\mathbb R$**: their <dot product> with $b$ is indeed $d$. Since $b\cdot c=1$ forces $c\ne0$, this is an entire affine <line>, not a single point. These alternatives are the <solvability of an affine rank-one vector equation>.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the integral for $B(q,p)$, put $u=1-x$, so $dx=-du$ and the endpoints are interchanged. Then
$$
B(q,p)=\int_1^0(1-u)^{q-1}u^{p-1}(-du)
=\int_0^1u^{p-1}(1-u)^{q-1}du=\boxed{B(p,q)}.
$$
Thus exchanging the positive parameters leaves the <Euler beta function> unchanged. Positivity ensures convergence at both endpoints of this improper integral.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The increasing substitution $x=\sin^2\theta$ maps $0\leq\theta\leq\pi/2$ onto $0\leq x\leq1$, with $dx=2\sin\theta\cos\theta\,d\theta$. Since <sine> and <cosine> are nonnegative there, powers combine without a sign ambiguity:
$$
\boxed{B(p,q)=2\int_0^{\pi/2}\sin^{2p-1}\theta\cos^{2q-1}\theta\,d\theta.}
$$
This is the <trigonometric form of the beta function>. The powers at the endpoints are integrable because $p,q>0$.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write $B(n,n)=\int_0^1[x(1-x)]^{n-1}dx$ and set $v=2x-1$. Since $x(1-x)=(1-v^2)/4$ and $dx=dv/2$,
$$
B(n,n)=2^{1-2n}\int_{-1}^{1}(1-v^2)^{n-1}dv
=2^{2-2n}\int_0^1(1-v^2)^{n-1}dv.
$$
In the last integral put $u=v^2$, giving $dv=du/(2\sqrt u)$. This yields $B(n,n)=2^{1-2n}B(1/2,n)$, and part (a) interchanges the arguments. Hence
$$
\boxed{B(n,n)=\frac{B(n,1/2)}{2^{2n-1}}.}
$$
The <equal-argument beta integral> identity holds for every positive real $n$, not only positive integers.

= d
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Multiply the two defining <Gamma integrals>. For $p,q>0$ the nonnegative product integrand allows <Tonelli's theorem>:
$$
\Gamma(p)\Gamma(q)=\int_0^\infty\int_0^\infty x^{p-1}y^{q-1}e^{-(x+y)}dx\,dy.
$$
Use $r=x+y$, $u=x/(x+y)$, so $x=ru$, $y=r(1-u)$ and the positive quadrant becomes $r>0$, $0<u<1$. The <Jacobian determinant> has absolute value
$$
\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}u&r\\1-u&-r\end{pmatrix}\right|=r.
$$
After including this factor, the transformed integral separates:
$$
\left[\int_0^\infty r^{p+q-1}e^{-r}dr\right]
\left[\int_0^1u^{p-1}(1-u)^{q-1}du\right]
=\Gamma(p+q)B(p,q).
$$
This proves the <beta-gamma identity>,
$$
\boxed{\Gamma(p)\Gamma(q)=B(p,q)\Gamma(p+q).}
$$
All changes of variables are made inside the open positive domains; their boundaries do not contribute to the integrals.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <product substitution for a first-order equation> $v=xy$. The <product rule> gives $v'=xy'+y$, turning the equation into $v'=e^v$. Consequently $(e^{-v})'=-1$, so $e^{-v}=C-x$.

At $x=1$ the initial value gives $v=1$, hence $C=1+e^{-1}$. Recovering $y=v/x$ gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=-\frac{\ln(1+e^{-1}-x)}{x},\qquad 0<x<1+e^{-1}.}
$$
This is the maximal real graph <interval> containing the initial point: the logarithm requires $x<1+e^{-1}$, and the numerator has a nonzero limit at zero, so this <particular solution> cannot extend through zero. Differentiating $xy=-\ln(C-x)$ verifies $xy'+y=1/(C-x)=e^{xy}$.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Away from $x=-1$ the <separable differential equation> gives
$$
\frac{dy}{y}=\frac{x^2}{1+x^3}dx,\qquad
\ln|y|=\frac13\ln|1+x^3|+C.
$$
On the <interval> containing $x=1$, one has $x>-1$, so the positive initial value selects $y=K(1+x^3)^{1/3}$. Substitution of $y(1)=2$ gives $K=2^{2/3}$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(x)=2^{2/3}(1+x^3)^{1/3},\qquad x>-1.}
$$
The zero solution omitted by division by $y$ cannot satisfy the initial value. Although the selected solution tends to zero as $x\downarrow-1$, its <derivative> diverges there; it does not extend through that point as a classical <differentiable> solution.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Homogeneous equation.** Its <characteristic equation> is $r^2-r=r(r-1)=0$. The two independent solutions are $1$ and $e^x$, so
$$
\boxed{y_{\mathrm{hom}}(x)=C_1+C_2e^x.}
$$
**Forced equation.** A <particular solution> is $-x^2/2-x$, since its second <derivative> minus its first <derivative> equals $x$. Thus $y=C_1+C_2e^x-x^2/2-x$ and $y'=C_2e^x-x-1$. The <derivative> condition at one gives $C_2e-2=2$, so $C_2=4/e$. The value condition then gives $C_1+4-1/2-1=1$, so $C_1=-3/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=4e^{x-1}-\frac{x^2}{2}-x-\frac32,\qquad x\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Both <initial conditions> and the forced equation follow directly on substitution.

= 18
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Put $s=b^at^ax$ and write $y'$ for <differentiation> with respect to $s$. Work on a real branch where the powers are defined and <differentiable>; $b>0$, $t>0$ suffices for arbitrary real $a$, and the parameter choice below has $b>0$. At fixed $x$, $\partial s/\partial t=as/t$. The <product rule> and <chain rule> therefore give
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}=at^{a-1}[y(s)+s y'(s)].}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At fixed $t$, the argument is linear in $x$, with $\partial s/\partial x=b^at^a$. Each <differentiation> therefore contributes this same constant factor, while the outside factor $t^a$ remains unchanged. Thus
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial^3u}{\partial x^3}=t^a(b^at^a)^3y^{(3)}(s)=b^{3a}t^{4a}y^{(3)}(s).}
$$
Here $y^{(3)}$ denotes the third <derivative> of the single-variable profile, not a partial <derivative> of $u$.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiate the <Airy ordinary differential equation> $y''=sy$ to obtain $y^{(3)}=y+sy'$. Combining part (a)'s <derivatives> yields
$$
u_t+u_{xxx}=[at^{a-1}+b^{3a}t^{4a}][y(s)+sy'(s)].
$$
To make this vanish for an arbitrary Airy profile, the time powers must agree: $a-1=4a$, giving $a=-1/3$. Their common <coefficient> must then vanish, giving $-1/3+b^{-1}=0$, hence $b=3$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a=-\frac13,\qquad b=3,\qquad
u(x,t)=t^{-1/3}y\left(\frac{x}{(3t)^{1/3}}\right).}
$$
For $t>0$ the two terms cancel exactly:
$$
u_t=-\frac13t^{-4/3}(y+sy'),\qquad
u_{xxx}=\frac13t^{-4/3}(y+sy').
$$
This is an <Airy similarity solution of the linear dispersive equation>. The <Airy equation> here is the linear partial <differential equation> $u_t+u_{xxx}=0$, the reduced equation specified in the question; it is distinct from the profile's <Airy ordinary differential equation>. The construction works for every linear combination of its two independent profiles, including zero. No condition at $t=0$ is supplied, and this singular time is excluded. With real cube roots the final formula can also be used separately on $t<0$.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

If $P\,dx+Q\,dy=dF$ with continuous second <partial derivatives> of $F$, then $P=F_x$, $Q=F_y$. The <symmetry of second derivatives> requires
$$
\boxed{P_y=Q_x.}
$$
This is a necessary condition for an <exact differential>. With continuously <differentiable> <coefficients> it is also locally sufficient, and is globally sufficient on a simply connected open domain; a domain with holes can require additional global information.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <partial derivatives> are $f_x=e^{xy}(1+xy)$ and $f_y=x^2e^{xy}$. At $(1,1)$ they have values $2e$ and $e$. The first-order <multivariate Taylor polynomial> therefore gives the change
$$
\boxed{\delta f=2e\,\delta x+e\,\delta y+O(\delta x^2+\delta y^2).}
$$
Thus the requested linear approximation is $e(2\delta x+\delta y)$; it is the change from $f(1,1)=e$, not the new <function> value itself.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Seek a separated nonzero solution $u=X(x)Y(y)$. Division by $XY$ on intervals where the factors are nonzero gives $X''/X+Y''/Y=-25$. Write $X''+\lambda X=0$ and $Y''+(25-\lambda)Y=0$, with both factors zero at their endpoints $0,\pi$.

For $X$, a nonpositive $\lambda$ gives only the zero solution under the two endpoint conditions: linear <functions> vanish identically when $\lambda=0$, and the hyperbolic solutions do likewise for $\lambda<0$. For $\lambda>0$, the endpoints force $X=A\sin(mx)$ with positive integer $m$ and $\lambda=m^2$. Applying the same argument to $Y$ gives $Y=B\sin(ny)$ and $25-m^2=n^2$, with positive integer $n$.

The only positive integer pairs satisfying $m^2+n^2=25$ are $(3,4)$ and $(4,3)$. By <superposition>,
$$
\boxed{u(x,y)=A\sin3x\sin4y+B\sin4x\sin3y,\qquad A,B\in\mathbb R.}
$$
To establish that this is the most general sufficiently regular solution, expand $u$ in the complete product <Fourier sine series> on the square. Substitution, or <integration> by parts against each <sine> mode, gives $(25-m^2-n^2)c_{mn}=0$ for every <coefficient>. Hence only the two displayed modes remain; all boundary terms vanish because of the given zero values. This is the eigenvalue-$25$ case of the <Dirichlet eigensystem of a rectangle>. The zero solution is included when $A=B=0$.

\Image[paper-1-square-eigenmodes.png]
{title=The two independent zero-boundary modes sharing Laplacian eigenvalue 25 on the square}

