{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $a_n=2^n/n!$. The <ratio test> gives $a_{n+1}/a_n=2/(n+1)\to0<1$, so **the series converges absolutely**. In fact the <exponential series> identifies its value as
$$
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{2^n}{n!}=e^2-1,
$$
with the $n=0$ term subtracted.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $0<\ln2<1$, so $(\ln2)^{-n}$ grows exponentially rather than decaying. The positive terms $a_n=n^3/(\ln2)^n$ do not tend to zero; indeed they tend to $+\infty$. Therefore **the series diverges** by the necessary condition that the <terms of a convergent series tend to zero>.

The <ratio test> gives the same conclusion: $a_{n+1}/a_n=((n+1)/n)^3/\ln2\to1/\ln2>1$.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For <absolute convergence>, examine $\sum_{n\geq1}|x-3|^n/n$. When $0<|x-3|<1$, the ratio of successive terms tends to $|x-3|<1$, so the <ratio test> proves convergence; at $x=3$ every term is zero. For $|x-3|>1$, the terms do not tend to zero.

At either boundary $|x-3|=1$, the absolute-value series is the divergent <harmonic series>. Thus for real $x$ the complete answer is
$$
\boxed{|x-3|<1,\qquad 2<x<4.}
$$
The original series at $x=2$ is conditionally convergent by the alternating-series criterion, but it is not absolutely convergent and is correctly excluded here.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The cosine-difference identity gives $\cos x-\cos3x=2\sin2x\sin x$. Hence
$$
\frac{\cos x-\cos3x}{x^2}
=4\frac{\sin2x}{2x}\frac{\sin x}{x}.
$$
Each <sine> ratio tends to one, so
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\cos x-\cos3x}{x^2}=4.}
$$
Equivalently, the quadratic terms of the two <cosine> expansions differ by $4x^2$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Near zero, $\cos x=1-x^2/2+O(x^4)$ and $\ln(1+v)=v+O(v^2)$, so $\ln\cos x=-x^2/2+O(x^4)$. Replacing $x$ by $3x$ similarly gives $\ln\cos3x=-9x^2/2+O(x^4)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\ln\cos x}{\ln\cos3x}=\frac19.}
$$
For all sufficiently small nonzero $x$, both cosines are positive and the denominator logarithm is nonzero, so this quotient and its two-sided real limit are well-defined.
